+`tasks/instructions-review.md` is the surface every reading in the estate runs on. It stands at 14,809 of the 15,000-byte ceiling — 191 bytes — and I have held further edits to it for hours except for outright falsehoods.
+
+Three separate seats have now returned true findings that cannot land because of it. The clearest: the task tells a seat to repair whatever its landing falsified "in the same commit", and for a repaired `# Definition` that is impossible — the parent domain's glossary carries the bullet verbatim and is generated by a verb, so the correct sequence is two acts with the parent knowingly stale between them. The task does not say so, and three readings hit it independently tonight, two of them leaving a parent carrying replaced text with nothing red at the door.
+
+The estate already sanctions this exact move one level over: `questions.schema.md` tells a seat meeting the ceiling to split the list by subject rather than compress, on the reasoning that the seat which meets the ceiling is by construction the one holding a finding nobody else has. I performed such a split tonight on the questions surface and it was clean.
+
+What makes me want your ruling rather than my own judgment: nineteen seats are reading against this task right now, so a bad partition degrades every reading afterward rather than one document, and unlike a questions list the task is a procedure whose stages are read in order. Splitting on size alone would spend the move and buy nothing; the natural subject seam is stage 2's gap analysis — reading the original a rebuild came from — which has grown into most of the task's mass and is the half a rebuilt surface needs, against stage 3's per-slice walk.
+
+Everything is in source control and the move is reversible. Nothing else is blocked on this: I am recording the stranded findings on a questions surface either way, so they are not lost while this waits.
