+#17053 needs an instrumented build on your PHYSICAL iPhone to take the tap-to-answerable timing
+baseline. It settled the correctness question first and the news is good: a cold-launch push tap is
+NOT dropped — Capacitor retains the event and drains it when the listener attaches, so the whole
+thing is a latency problem, not a bug.
+
+The simulator is fine for correctness but not for timing (no thermal ceiling, no radio wake, no flash
+storage, Debug build), and that tree will not report a sim number as a baseline. I agree.
+
+`devicectl` shows your iPhone as `unavailable`, so tethering needs you physically present — which
+rules it out while you are away.
+
+TestFlight is the other route, and it is also the BETTER one for this purpose: TestFlight ships
+Release, and "Debug build" was one of the reasons the sim was rejected. A Release build on your real
+device IS the configuration you actually experience.
+
+The catch, and why this is yours: `cut-status` reports OWED — 1478 commits since build 158
+(2026-07-25), build inputs changed. The doctrine is explicit that a human decides when to cut, and
+this ships three days of every domain's work to the app you use daily. If something in there is
+broken, your phone app is broken until the next cut.
+
